reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
Chance enters only through the setup permutation. Because every player has the identical multiset, the luck lies only in overlaps. If a rival's first pick is your 14 object (prob. about 1/6 per rival pick), you lose access to it. Estimated share of the outcome from chance: about 20-30%.

Under symmetric good play, the result is decided by:
- how much to shade bids in each first-price auction;
- when to save coins for the late rounds when rivals are depleted;
- reading rivals' remaining coins and object counts (the tie-break);
- inferring from picks and talk which objects rivals still value.
It is not decided by the deal alone.

Things to infer:
- rivals' values: hidden, but each pick signals the picker's high value for that object;
- rivals' likely bids, which are bounded by public coins;
- the credibility of talk.
These can be acted on through bid sizing and pick choice. A player who overbids early, or never bids, loses clearly to one who reasons about budgets.

The deal gives everyone the same multiset of values. Chance enters only through collisions: an opponent taking the object you value at 14 or 10, about 1/6 per opposing win for your top object. That typically costs at most 4 points (14 to 10). Picks are your own top, so the deal is largely symmetric.

What decides results:
- bid shading against publicly known coin totals;
- timing, i.e. spending early versus waiting to exploit depleted rivals;
- reading talk;
- inferring rivals' remaining top values from their picks.

Under symmetric good play, outcomes differ by bid choices and occasional collisions, not by the deal alone. Estimated chance share is about 20-25% of result variance. A reasoner who shades bids and times purchases beats a passer, an all-in bidder and a random bidder, as computed above.
